= Conclusion

This report set out to quantify the direct socio-economic impacts of flooding with methods that are open, global and fast enough to be used in the first days after an event. The main outcome is SEICHE, a Python pipeline that turns satellite imagery or a 2D hydrodynamic simulation into a hazard raster of depth, velocity and duration, then combines it with land cover and damage functions for the economic impact and with a population raster for the social impact. The St-Omer test case shows both sides of the approach. The flood extent is reasonably estimated (about 18 km² against 23 km² of ground truth), but the estimated building damage varies by a factor of more than 6 depending on the depth-estimation method (115 M€ to 750 M€), and the affected population ranges from 16,700 to 88,600 depending on the population dataset. Satellite-based impact estimation is therefore useful in niche conditions: it is the only method that is open, rapid and global, but its precision remains a major question mark. The second contribution, FSFS, explores how an agent-based model could generate social damage curves analogous to economic damage functions; it is a proof of concept, not a validated tool, and its results are presented as possibilities rather than scientific facts. The overall lesson is that these methods can now give orders of magnitude anywhere on Earth within days, but they cannot yet replace local data, hydrodynamic modelling or ground truth when precision matters.
== Limits

The interdisciplinarity of this topic and the plethora of datasets and methods available always lead to choices in the ones we consider in the limited scope of a technical report. Many more datasets and methods could be used: we chose the ones that seemed relevant and commonly in use in the state of the art, both worldwide and in France. Only a subset of the available hazard sources was used, while follow-up studies compare the results obtained from 2D hydrodynamic models, spatialized low-resolution 1D models and _in-situ_ data. This precision/rapidity trade-off is the main lesson of the St-Omer test case: the flood extent stays close to the ground truth, but the estimated damage varies by a factor of more than 6 with the depth-estimation method.

== Perspectives

Computing the socio-economic impacts of natural disasters is an emerging field of study: more and more researchers and governing entities want to obtain such insights, but the field is still in desperate need of research and improved methodology if we want our assessments to be more than rough estimates. But it also means that there is a plethora of perspectives to pursue. This part will be an anthology, which cannot possibly be fully exhaustive, of some of the plausible evolutions of research on this topic, based on the visible seams in the methodology that these three years of work, as well as the literature, showed.

On the software side, one obvious direction is to plug more pre- and post-processing directly into SEICHE, starting with FloodML.

=== Improved data sources

One way of improving the methodology is to provide better data sources.

Among all satellite missions for hydrology, SWOT is a promising one: its data have been assimilated for flood forecasting @bonas2026. Pléiades Neo offers a better optical resolution, which can lead to a better flood extent as well as a better land cover representation. CO3D (Constellation Optique 3D) would provide a better global DEM. Finally, some of the French datasets used in this report could be improved or replaced: OSO from CESBIO is one example, while OCSGE (Occupation du Sol à Grande Échelle) and LiDAR HD, both from the IGN, are alternatives that this report does not use.

=== New methods

In the same way that ABMs can be used to derive SDCs, they could also be used to create economic damage functions. Indeed, if simulated agents follow microeconomic rules, macroeconomic patterns may emerge that could give insights on the real impact of a flooding event, on the individuals but also on the public institutions, or companies. These rules have the advantage of being historically more studied and validated, and would need less effort in scientific validation to be put in place with confidence. It could even be coupled inside FSFS or equivalent, making it a compendious socio-economic damage curve generator.

Other directions are worth mentioning: economic input-output (I/O) tables, population dynamics throughout the day and the year, large language models (LLMs) for nomenclature joining, change detection, more refined damage functions, and natural language processing (NLP) applied to social networks.

=== Including more fields of study

Finally, the scope of this work was primarily to create bridges between remote sensing, 2D hydrodynamics, geography and socio-economic impact estimations, because of the sheer number of impacts and consequences flooding has, and because of the massive number of ways floodwater can be observed, modelled or inferred. However, even in this colossal scope, there are still some research fields that should be included in the discussions to have a more compendious view of the topic.

First, climate change. It is a surprise to no one that the increased energy in the Earth system is having unprecedented consequences that translate into more numerous, and more powerful, natural disasters. As mentioned in the context chapter, even if the future local dynamics are still not set in stone, there is a global consensus on the fact that floods and their effects are going to be a higher and higher concern in the future. Climate scientists should therefore be included in the loop if the goal is to understand how the impacts of flooding events will evolve in the future. For instance, the precipitation events of major and impactful storms, which led to flash floods and storm surges (CITE MARGOT), can serve as input for damage estimation tools, allowing us to understand the consequences of such events. These same precipitation events can be "futurized", that is, simulated in a future climate thanks to climate models (CITE LILIAN), and serve in the same way as input for damage estimation models. _Ceteris paribus_ -- all things being equal, that is DEM, land cover, damage functions, etc. -- it could help quantify the cost of climate inaction. If all other parameters are equal, and if the future estimation leads to a 50% increase in the economic cost for a given event, then it gives insights and political motivations for governing (and other) entities to take action in order to tackle the greenhouse gas issue in our atmosphere and the lack of protection measures for its consequences. It can also be used as input for hydrodynamic models like Torrent.jl @torrentjl, which feed into FSFS and the SDC building, allowing us to quantify, in terms of human lives, the cost of climate change.

However, the consequences we can draw from such tools are as of now still uncertain. As heavily mentioned and repeated in the FSFS part of this report, no scientific validation was done, and the computed curves should only serve as promises of a possible application rather than established facts. But these promises are bright, once sociologists get in the loop and allow us to remove scientific locks one by one. It is hard to know how far such models could be pushed, since the field of computational sociology is still emerging -- probably not to the point of the psychohistory equations from Isaac Asimov's Foundation series, but enough to build resilient and proven social damage curves for natural disasters.

Finally, while we only look at death as the ultimate social metric to quantify the impact of a disaster, it is not the only one. Physical and mental trauma induced directly by the water, or the derived sickness, forced displacement, unemployment, lack of access to health providers and public services, lower food production, and so on, are more factors that should be taken into account. It requires sociology expertise, as mentioned before, but also health professionals, doctors, epidemiologists, psychologists, social workers, and more care professions.
